1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,393
Good morning, I'm Whale Girl.

2
00:00:02,413 --> 00:00:04,750
Today is October 13.

3
00:00:04,770 --> 00:00:06,053
Six stories today:

4
00:00:06,073 --> 00:00:08,236
Meta releases its first frontier model,

5
00:00:08,256 --> 00:00:12,293
GPT-6.1 Sol's default context grows to 500k,

6
00:00:12,313 --> 00:00:15,176
Microsoft unveils the Maia 300 chip,

7
00:00:15,196 --> 00:00:17,676
AlphaProof Nexus lands in Science,

8
00:00:17,696 --> 00:00:19,562
clearing chat history

9
00:00:19,582 --> 00:00:22,156
helps agents find better ideas,

10
00:00:22,176 --> 00:00:24,212
and Qwen's QwenGyre arrives.

11
00:00:24,232 --> 00:00:25,990
First, Meta has released

12
00:00:26,010 --> 00:00:27,768
its first frontier model,

13
00:00:27,788 --> 00:00:29,556
called Muse Wonder.

14
00:00:29,576 --> 00:00:30,692
There are two versions:

15
00:00:30,712 --> 00:00:33,877
Muse Wonder and Muse Wonder Full.

16
00:00:33,897 --> 00:00:35,717
Both models support a 1 million

17
00:00:35,737 --> 00:00:37,223
token context

18
00:00:37,243 --> 00:00:41,473
and accept text, images, video and audio.

19
00:00:41,493 --> 00:00:43,976
Muse Wonder is available through Meta's API,

20
00:00:43,996 --> 00:00:45,558
per million tokens,

21
00:00:45,578 --> 00:00:48,174
at $4 for input and $20 for output.

22
00:00:48,194 --> 00:00:50,765
The Full version is for Muse Code subscribers only,

23
00:00:50,785 --> 00:00:53,742
not served through the API, and has no public price.

24
00:00:53,762 --> 00:00:54,828
According to Meta's blog,

25
00:00:54,848 --> 00:00:57,268
the models were trained in central Ohio,

26
00:00:57,288 --> 00:00:59,570
at the Prometheus data center.

27
00:00:59,590 --> 00:01:00,846
Meta's own tests say

28
00:01:00,866 --> 00:01:02,706
both models and Fable 5.1 and

29
00:01:02,726 --> 00:01:06,266
GPT-6 Astra have comparable benchmark scores.

30
00:01:06,286 --> 00:01:10,033
On the third-party Artificial Analysis

31
00:01:10,053 --> 00:01:13,020
Intelligence Index, the Full version scores 56,

32
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:16,697
versus 48 for the previous Muse Spark 1.3.

33
00:01:16,717 --> 00:01:19,263
But on the AA-Omniscience knowledge test,

34
00:01:19,283 --> 00:01:21,220
the Full version scores only 30,

35
00:01:21,240 --> 00:01:24,353
while Claude Opus 5.5 scores 46.

36
00:01:24,373 --> 00:01:25,418
Muse Wonder will

37
00:01:25,438 --> 00:01:27,634
also come to Meta's Muse app,

38
00:01:27,654 --> 00:01:29,991
starting with paid subscribers.

39
00:01:30,011 --> 00:01:31,385
The cost per task

40
00:01:31,405 --> 00:01:33,229
is also about three times the previous generation's.

41
00:01:33,249 --> 00:01:34,700
TechCrunch notes

42
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:38,090
that Meta's comparisons come from its own model card.

43
00:01:38,290 --> 00:01:40,142
Second, OpenAI has raised

44
00:01:40,162 --> 00:01:44,106
the default context length of GPT-6.1 Sol

45
00:01:44,126 --> 00:01:48,034
from 272k to 500k, about 80% more.

46
00:01:48,054 --> 00:01:49,364
The change applies in the Codex

47
00:01:49,384 --> 00:01:51,220
and Work environments.

48
00:01:51,240 --> 00:01:53,789
IT Home says, when using subscription quota,

49
00:01:53,809 --> 00:01:57,003
Sol carries no extra long-context fee.

50
00:01:57,023 --> 00:01:59,554
OpenAI's Thibault Sottiaux says,

51
00:01:59,574 --> 00:02:01,894
the value is well tested,

52
00:02:01,914 --> 00:02:03,774
for most users,

53
00:02:03,794 --> 00:02:06,477
it is the best balance between experience and performance.

54
00:02:06,497 --> 00:02:09,434
Sol supports a maximum context of 1M,

55
00:02:09,454 --> 00:02:12,308
and users can raise or lower it themselves.

56
00:02:12,328 --> 00:02:13,972
Adjusting it can reduce how often context

57
00:02:13,992 --> 00:02:15,584
compression is triggered.

58
00:02:15,604 --> 00:02:19,355
OpenAI also says it will keep raising the limit.

59
00:02:19,375 --> 00:02:21,781
The article does not mention specific pricing.

60
00:02:21,981 --> 00:02:23,956
Third, Microsoft unveils

61
00:02:23,976 --> 00:02:27,160
its in-house AI chip, Maia 300.

62
00:02:27,180 --> 00:02:29,479
Maia 200 already powers,

63
00:02:29,499 --> 00:02:31,096
in the US, Europe and Australia,

64
00:02:31,116 --> 00:02:33,546
Microsoft Copilot.

65
00:02:33,566 --> 00:02:37,022
Each chip has 288GB of HBM4 memory,

66
00:02:37,042 --> 00:02:41,332
18TB/s of bandwidth and a rated power of 950 watts.

67
00:02:41,352 --> 00:02:43,273
One rack can hold 96 chips.

68
00:02:43,293 --> 00:02:46,257
Microsoft says, per-megawatt token output,

69
00:02:46,277 --> 00:02:49,205
is 40% higher than leading GPU systems.

70
00:02:49,225 --> 00:02:51,578
A 96-chip rack, token output,

71
00:02:51,598 --> 00:02:54,070
is up to 7 times a Maia 200 rack's.

72
00:02:54,090 --> 00:02:56,966
But the article doesn't say which GPU or how it was tested,

73
00:02:56,986 --> 00:03:00,142
and gives no benchmarks or pricing.

74
00:03:00,162 --> 00:03:02,288
Maia 300 targets

75
00:03:02,308 --> 00:03:05,426
long inputs, fast conversations and agent tasks.

76
00:03:05,446 --> 00:03:07,427
Starting in 2027, Microsoft plans

77
00:03:07,447 --> 00:03:09,367
multi-gigawatt deployment.

78
00:03:09,387 --> 00:03:13,095
More details will come at the Ignite conference in November.

79
00:03:13,295 --> 00:03:15,318
Fourth, Google DeepMind's

80
00:03:15,338 --> 00:03:16,766
AlphaProof Nexus has

81
00:03:16,786 --> 00:03:19,402
had its results published in Science.

82
00:03:19,422 --> 00:03:20,981
It uses Gemini 3.1 Pro

83
00:03:21,001 --> 00:03:22,618
to draft proofs in the Lean formal language,

84
00:03:22,638 --> 00:03:24,859
Lean checks every revision,

85
00:03:24,879 --> 00:03:27,195
and its errors guide the next round.

86
00:03:27,215 --> 00:03:28,572
Harder subproblems

87
00:03:28,592 --> 00:03:32,145
call the dedicated proof tool AlphaProof.

88
00:03:32,165 --> 00:03:34,976
Of 353 unsolved Erdos problems,

89
00:03:34,996 --> 00:03:36,528
it solved nine,

90
00:03:36,548 --> 00:03:39,106
two of which had been open for 56 years.

91
00:03:39,126 --> 00:03:41,382
According to the paper, without AlphaProof's help,

92
00:03:41,402 --> 00:03:43,896
the system also solved these nine problems.

93
00:03:43,916 --> 00:03:45,527
Before the paper was published,

94
00:03:45,547 --> 00:03:47,908
the team had already posted the solutions online.

95
00:03:47,928 --> 00:03:49,954
It also proved 44 conjectures,

96
00:03:49,974 --> 00:03:51,649
from the OEIS sequence database,

97
00:03:51,669 --> 00:03:54,939
and settled an open problem in algebraic geometry.

98
00:03:54,959 --> 00:03:56,733
The Decoder's contrast:

99
00:03:56,753 --> 00:03:58,584
OpenAI recently uploaded hundreds of proofs

100
00:03:58,604 --> 00:04:00,175
to GitHub,

101
00:04:00,195 --> 00:04:03,140
DeepMind went through peer review.

102
00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:05,795
The article does not list any limitations of the study.

103
00:04:05,995 --> 00:04:08,946
Fifth, a Microsoft Research paper says,

104
00:04:08,966 --> 00:04:11,258
for long-running agents,

105
00:04:11,278 --> 00:04:12,220
the longer they chat,

106
00:04:12,240 --> 00:04:14,586
the more easily they get stuck in one line of thinking.

107
00:04:14,606 --> 00:04:18,107
They call this phenomenon

108
00:04:18,127 --> 00:04:20,663
"idea basins."

109
00:04:20,683 --> 00:04:22,474
The method is called Fork-and-Flush.

110
00:04:22,494 --> 00:04:24,675
It first splits into K parallel branches,

111
00:04:24,695 --> 00:04:27,743
and each branch inherits the workspace and files.

112
00:04:27,763 --> 00:04:30,697
Then each branch clears all its chat context

113
00:04:30,717 --> 00:04:32,543
and continues from a fresh conversation,

114
00:04:32,563 --> 00:04:34,888
and only the best branch is kept.

115
00:04:34,908 --> 00:04:37,537
In other words, it drops the chat history

116
00:04:37,557 --> 00:04:39,774
but keeps the code and files.

117
00:04:39,794 --> 00:04:42,955
On 13 long-running tasks with the same compute,

118
00:04:42,975 --> 00:04:46,922
its average normalized score is 0.78,

119
00:04:46,942 --> 00:04:49,655
versus 0.47 for a single run.

120
00:04:49,675 --> 00:04:52,868
That is 66% higher than a single run

121
00:04:52,888 --> 00:04:56,316
and 44.4% higher than best-of-4.

122
00:04:56,336 --> 00:04:57,992
Clearing the chat without forking

123
00:04:58,012 --> 00:05:02,082
also gave about a 22% gain on 11 tasks.

124
00:05:02,102 --> 00:05:04,583
Note that the experiment covers only 13 tasks,

125
00:05:04,603 --> 00:05:08,655
uses GPT-5.6-terra and GPT-5.5,

126
00:05:08,675 --> 00:05:11,443
and forking costs extra compute.

127
00:05:11,643 --> 00:05:14,754
Sixth, Alibaba's Qwen has released QwenGyre,

128
00:05:14,774 --> 00:05:16,891
an online reinforcement learning framework,

129
00:05:16,911 --> 00:05:19,084
for long-horizon agent tasks.

130
00:05:19,104 --> 00:05:21,520
Qwen says a complex agent job

131
00:05:21,540 --> 00:05:23,875
can run for hours,

132
00:05:23,895 --> 00:05:25,160
with hundreds of interactions

133
00:05:25,180 --> 00:05:27,340
and over a million tokens processed in total.

134
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:29,189
QwenGyre uses elastic scheduling:

135
00:05:29,209 --> 00:05:30,476
each time a batch of tasks finishes,

136
00:05:30,496 --> 00:05:32,906
the freed-up GPUs switch to training.

137
00:05:32,926 --> 00:05:36,207
It also organizes trajectories into a tree by shared prefix

138
00:05:36,227 --> 00:05:38,574
and expands only the selected paths.

139
00:05:38,594 --> 00:05:42,735
In Qwen's experiment on NL2RepoBench,

140
00:05:42,755 --> 00:05:45,162
with about 700,000 tokens per rollout

141
00:05:45,182 --> 00:05:46,279
and 48 training steps,

142
00:05:46,299 --> 00:05:49,280
the pass rate rose from 52.5% to 58.5%.

143
00:05:49,300 --> 00:05:51,020
With the same GPU budget,

144
00:05:51,040 --> 00:05:53,985
it is 1.78 times faster end to end than Async.

145
00:05:54,005 --> 00:05:57,176
Qwen also says Qwen3.6-122B sees

146
00:05:57,196 --> 00:05:59,916
end-to-end speedups on three kinds of tasks,

147
00:05:59,936 --> 00:06:02,716
but the article gives no figures.

148
00:06:02,736 --> 00:06:05,397
These are Qwen's own figures from specific setups,

149
00:06:05,417 --> 00:06:07,408
the article doesn't say if it is open source,

150
00:06:07,428 --> 00:06:09,542
or how to use it.

151
00:06:09,742 --> 00:06:12,838
That's all for today. Sources are in the description.

152
00:06:12,858 --> 00:06:14,142
See you tomorrow.
